i don't know about you but i can never  get enough of minimalist money-saving  tips tricks and hacks and with this  crazy inflation what a better time to  focus on improving your finances and  reducing expenses than now so today i  will share with you 12 money-saving tips  that i plan to use to save more money in  2023 but make sure you stick to the end  because i have saved the best one for  last so number one is before purchasing  anything after you ask do i need this  also ask this question can i diy this  for example i recently needed a planner  because i want to plan out my daughter's  homeschool calendar i browsed briefly  through the internet and then i realized  i have a lot of notebooks in the home  and so i got out my handy-dandy ruler  and a pen and  i made my own  if you ask this question before you go  to buy anything you may be surprised at  just how many things you can do much  cheaper if you just do it yourself  number two is prior to tossing or  replacing your clothing consider how you  can alter it to make it work for you so  for example i had a dress that i wasn't  too fond of the sleeves so i cut them  off and i made them a bit shorter i had  two high-waisted dress pants that i  realized i don't need two of them so i  cut one of them and made them into  shorts because i didn't have shorts at  that time by doing this i saved myself  from purchasing new items prior to  tossing an item consider how you can  mend it to make it work for you and if  you aren't able to do so then consider  possibly having someone do this for you  it tends to be much cheaper than  actually purchasing a brand new item and  number three is if you just thought to  yourself well i don't know how to sew  then this is for you learn new skills  such as cooking sewing and gardening  these skills can be huge money savers  because as i mentioned through sewing  your clothing instead of having to  replace it you can just mend that or  even gardening you can have fresh fruits  and vegetables or you can freeze things  such as what we do learning these new  skills doesn't have to be even  complicated at all because you have tons  of videos at your disposal on youtube  for example and it's totally free and in  the case of sewing the kit that i have  is very small it takes up very minimal  space but i have saved quite a few  stuffed animals with these so make it a  goal to learn one new skill per season  and then the next season you can focus  on another skill and then if things hit  the fan you have a skill where you could  make things to be able to sell to other  people to make more money and who  doesn't love more money right so for  example i can knit and i can make  scarves and things like that i don't  enjoy it but i know that i have that  skill and should i ever need to sell  these sorts of things i can do so number  four is huge and it is to replace your  social media scrolling with youtube  videos podcasts or audiobooks that are  relevant to better managing your  finances minimalism or just saving money  in general think about it you can either  spend your time scrolling through  instagram watching what the kardashians  are doing or what other people are  wearing and what they have and what they  own and think about the things you lack  the things that you need more of or you  can listen to content or watch content  that is motivating you to own less to  desire less to free you from that  shopping addiction that you may or may  not have and also at the same time  motivating you to save more money to  learn how to better manage your finances  swapping this out can be huge i know  that for me when i purchased dave  ramsey's book the total money makeover  it was a huge life changer for me and i  got so motivated that on my commutes i  would listen to podcasts about saving  money and i would watch youtube videos  on that topic and i learned so much that  i hadn't before and this is kind of what  led me into minimalism but it just i  think goes kind of hand in hand so be  really cautious about the kind of  content that you are consuming and make  sure that it's something that's going to  actually help you get towards your goal  and tip number five is to have a lazy  quick go-to meal we've all heard of meal  planning as part of saving money  sometimes life just doesn't work out  that way you have a hectic day and then  you come home and you just don't have  the time to be able to make a  well-rounded dinner for those days have  a quick meal that you can just whip up  in 10 minutes what we do is we have some  shiitake noodles which don't need to be  cooked they just come in a bag prepared  some frozen veggies that we just always  keep in stock in the freezer some eggs  and some coconut aminos which is  basically the soy free version of soy  sauce and ta-da we have a stir-fry in  under 10 minutes  just make sure that you always have the  ingredients at hand so that on those  occasions when you have a crazy hectic  day you can just whip this up really  quick number six is also food related  and it is intermittent fasting or if you  just don't want to do intermittent  fasting just don't snack throughout the  day this is actually not something that  i've really heard about in terms of  saving money but it's one of the effects  that i have noticed through intermittent  fasting throughout the years and i've  talked a lot about intermittent fasting  and especially through my videos on my  experience with the keto diet which i  can link down below every time you eat  you're spiking your insulin levels when  you reduce your insulin levels you can  decrease inflammation in the body it  means that i am eating healthier meals i  no longer binge eat and i'm not having  to cook or prepare buying all of these  things in my home matter of fact we  don't really keep things like cereals or  cookies or chips or any of those sorts  of snacks in our home the snacks that we  do have are for our kids really and my  husband but it tends to be things like  fruits and those sorts of things  reducing the amount of snacking or  trying out intermittent fasting can  really save you a lot of money in the  long run but it can be so good for your  health however make sure that it's okay  with your doctor or nutritionist before  you try anything like that because i'm  not a doctor i am not a nutritionist  this is just what has worked for me and  i have noticed that has saved me money  as well number seven is to have a wish  list and i don't mean the wish list  where you tell people i would love it if  you would give me this not that kind of  thing although you can totally do that  if you want to by this i mean having an  area where you can jot down the things  that you would like to buy but you don't  really need or don't really need them  right away so for example you can jot  things down on a notebook or have it on  your notes app on your phone because i  don't want to just go to the store and  buy whatever with the high dollar price  tag i want to maybe shop the sales or  find a used version so by doing this i'm  kind of also giving myself the time  where i don't have to just buy it right  now because of the fomo and then at the  same time you may decide that some of  those items you jotted on your list you  don't really want them anymore and  number eight is for those items that you  want to purchase online so before you  actually click the buy now button make  sure to give yourself at least 24 to 48  hours with that item in your cart i have  done this a lot where i'm thinking about  an item that i'm going to purchase and  i'll just have it sitting there in my  cart for a few days because i know that  way i'm not going to forget about it or  i'm not going to have to search through  all the items to remember what item i  specifically wanted but at the same time  it gives me the opportunity to really  think about it do i really need it and  if i forget about it that's a clue that  i really did not 